# The Encyclopedia's concept articles (ui/pedia/concepts.rs). One message per title and paragraph.

## sections of the contents list
classic.pedia.concepts.part-economy = Economy and society
classic.pedia.concepts.part-empire = Culture, trade, diplomacy and victory
classic.pedia.concepts.part-combat = Combat and movement

## Turn Order
classic.pedia.concepts.turn-order.title = Turn Order
classic.pedia.concepts.turn-order.p1 = During your turn, give orders to units, choose what cities produce, and review research and diplomacy. Orders can change the map immediately, so inspect their result before giving the next order.
classic.pedia.concepts.turn-order.p2 = End Turn passes play onward. Income, research, production and other ongoing activities advance as turns are processed. Review notifications and the updated city and empire panels when control returns to you.
classic.pedia.concepts.turn-order.p3 = Skip leaves a unit idle for this turn. Sleep keeps it out of the normal order cycle until you return to it. Neither replaces the need to manage your cities and other choices.

## City Growth
classic.pedia.concepts.city-growth.title = City Growth
classic.pedia.concepts.city-growth.p1 = Food supports a city's population. A surplus fills its growth progress; a shortage can consume stored food and lead to starvation. The city panel shows stored food, the growth requirement and the current outlook.
classic.pedia.concepts.city-growth.p2 = Open the city and review its citizens, worked land and specialists. Food-rich tiles can help a growing city, while production or specialist assignments may suit a different goal. City focus helps direct automatic assignments; locked assignments let you keep a deliberate choice.
classic.pedia.concepts.city-growth.p3 = Growth changes as the city and its bonuses change. Use the city's current figures when deciding whether to add citizens, improve land or redirect existing workers.

## Border Growth
classic.pedia.concepts.border-growth.title = Border Growth
classic.pedia.concepts.border-growth.p1 = A city's territory can expand through culture. Owned land determines where you can work tiles and make many improvements, but ownership does not automatically mean that a citizen is working every tile.
classic.pedia.concepts.border-growth.p2 = Inspect the city's territory and citizen assignments together. A newly acquired resource may still need the appropriate technology and improvement before it benefits your empire.
classic.pedia.concepts.border-growth.p3 = If you need a particular eligible tile sooner, use Buy a tile in the city screen instead of waiting for cultural expansion.

## Buying Tiles
classic.pedia.concepts.buying-tiles.title = Buying Tiles
classic.pedia.concepts.buying-tiles.p1 = Open one of your cities and choose Buy a tile. The map highlights eligible land and shows its current gold price. Select a highlighted tile to review it, then use the purchase confirmation to spend gold.
classic.pedia.concepts.buying-tiles.p2 = Selecting a tile alone does not buy it. Unaffordable or otherwise unavailable choices explain why they cannot be purchased. Prices and eligibility can change, so use the displayed offer rather than assuming that neighboring tiles cost the same.
classic.pedia.concepts.buying-tiles.p3 = Finish buying to return to city management. Pressing Escape leaves purchase mode before leaving the city screen.

## Production and Purchase Costs
classic.pedia.concepts.production-and-purchase-costs.title = Production and Purchase Costs
classic.pedia.concepts.production-and-purchase-costs.p1 = Cities use production to complete their selected units, buildings and projects. Add an available item to the production queue; the current item shows its progress and an estimate when the city has production to contribute.
classic.pedia.concepts.production-and-purchase-costs.p2 = Use search and the production categories to find an item. Available choices can be separated from locked ones; inspect a locked choice to understand its requirements. Completing a technology or building may open new choices.
classic.pedia.concepts.production-and-purchase-costs.p3 = Where a purchase or hurry action is offered, review its displayed cost and availability before using it. Production requirements and purchase prices are different, and bonuses can change the values shown for a particular city.

## Gold
classic.pedia.concepts.gold.title = Gold
classic.pedia.concepts.gold.p1 = Gold in the treasury pays for purchases and other immediate expenses. Income per turn is separate from the treasury: a positive balance adds gold over time, while a deficit draws it down.
classic.pedia.concepts.gold.p2 = Review your economy before adding recurring commitments. Units, buildings, routes and diplomatic agreements can affect the balance; a large treasury can hide a continuing deficit for a while.
classic.pedia.concepts.gold.p3 = City yields, the empire overview and trade offers show different parts of your economy. Check both the immediate price and any ongoing payment when comparing choices.

## Happiness
classic.pedia.concepts.happiness.title = Happiness
classic.pedia.concepts.happiness.p1 = Happiness reflects how well your empire supports its population. Expanding and growing cities can increase its demands; luxuries, buildings and other effects can help meet them.
classic.pedia.concepts.happiness.p2 = Watch happiness alongside growth and expansion. A promising new city may also need support from your economy, improved resources or suitable buildings.
classic.pedia.concepts.happiness.p3 = Inspect the current happiness information and the effects listed on buildings, resources and policies. Do not assume that a duplicate resource or another copy of a building always provides the same empire-wide benefit.

## Golden Ages
classic.pedia.concepts.golden-ages.title = Golden Ages
classic.pedia.concepts.golden-ages.p1 = A Golden Age is a temporary period of improved economic output. Its effects can depend on your nation and the bonuses you have acquired.
classic.pedia.concepts.golden-ages.p2 = Some great people and other effects can start or extend a Golden Age. Read the available action and its consequences before consuming a great person that could serve another purpose.
classic.pedia.concepts.golden-ages.p3 = Use the current city and empire yields to see its benefit. When a temporary bonus ends, check whether purchases, production plans or recurring expenses still fit your economy.

## Social Policies
classic.pedia.concepts.social-policies.title = Social Policies
classic.pedia.concepts.social-policies.p1 = Culture allows you to adopt social policies. Open Social Policies to inspect branches, their prerequisites and the effects of each available policy.
classic.pedia.concepts.social-policies.p2 = Opening a branch and adopting policies within it are separate decisions. Some choices require an era or earlier policies; unavailable choices explain what is missing.
classic.pedia.concepts.social-policies.p3 = Ideologies add later choices and can interact with cultural influence from other nations. Review public opinion in the Culture screen before considering an ideology change.

## Science
classic.pedia.concepts.science.title = Science
classic.pedia.concepts.science.p1 = Science advances your chosen research. Open the technology tree to see known technologies, the current research and what each technology unlocks.
classic.pedia.concepts.science.p2 = Select an unknown technology to plan a research path to it. Missing prerequisites are placed before the target, shared prerequisites appear once, and technologies already known are skipped. Selecting another target replaces the plan while keeping accumulated science.
classic.pedia.concepts.science.p3 = When you have a free technology choice, choose from the eligible options first. Use the displayed cost and research estimate for your nation rather than treating every technology's base cost as the amount you must currently pay.

## Great People
classic.pedia.concepts.great-people.title = Great People
classic.pedia.concepts.great-people.p1 = Great people offer specialized actions. Their available orders depend on their kind and situation: examples include creating an improvement, helping research or production, conducting a trade mission, or creating a Great Work.
classic.pedia.concepts.great-people.p2 = City specialists and other effects can contribute toward great person progress. Inspect the city's specialist assignments and progress before shifting citizens away from worked tiles.
classic.pedia.concepts.great-people.p3 = Many great person actions consume the unit. Compare its available uses and check the action's requirements before committing it.

## Faith and Religion
classic.pedia.concepts.faith-and-religion.title = Faith and Religion
classic.pedia.concepts.faith-and-religion.p1 = Faith supports religious choices and eligible purchases. Open Religion to review your faith, available pantheon or religion choices, beliefs and purchase options.
classic.pedia.concepts.faith-and-religion.p2 = Beliefs define a religion's effects. Founding, enhancing and reforming a religion have different requirements; choose from the options the screen currently allows.
classic.pedia.concepts.faith-and-religion.p3 = Religious units can spread religion or perform other specialized actions. Select the unit to inspect its orders and requirements. A city's religious state can change as religions spread, so revisit its information when planning religious buildings or purchases.

## Score and the End of the Game
classic.pedia.concepts.score-and-the-end-of-the-game.title = Score and the End of the Game
classic.pedia.concepts.score-and-the-end-of-the-game.p1 = Score summarizes aspects of your empire, but a high score does not replace the requirements of the other victory conditions. Open Victory to compare progress and the scores available to you.
classic.pedia.concepts.score-and-the-end-of-the-game.p2 = The match settings determine which victory conditions and turn limit apply. If time victory is enabled, the end-of-game score matters when that limit is reached.
classic.pedia.concepts.score-and-the-end-of-the-game.p3 = The results screen identifies the outcome. Continuing for another turn lets you keep playing after a completed result; it does not undo that recorded outcome.

## Tourism and Influence
classic.pedia.concepts.tourism-and-influence.title = Tourism and Influence
classic.pedia.concepts.tourism-and-influence.p1 = Tourism builds cultural influence over other nations. Their accumulated culture matters to that relationship, so producing tourism and resisting another nation's influence are different tasks.
classic.pedia.concepts.tourism-and-influence.p2 = Open Culture to review influence levels, trends and the nations on which you have become influential. Great Works, buildings and other effects can contribute to tourism.
classic.pedia.concepts.tourism-and-influence.p3 = Cultural victory depends on influence over the other nations required by the current game. Cultural pressure can also affect ideological public opinion; check that information before changing ideology.

## Great Works and Archaeology
classic.pedia.concepts.great-works-and-archaeology.title = Great Works and Archaeology
classic.pedia.concepts.great-works-and-archaeology.p1 = Great Works occupy compatible slots in buildings. Writers, artists and musicians can create their respective kinds of work when the action is available. Archaeology can provide artifacts through excavation choices.
classic.pedia.concepts.great-works-and-archaeology.p2 = Open Culture to see which buildings hold works and which slots are empty. Each recorded work shows its kind, originating nation and era. The illustration helps identify it; it is not a claim that the game stores a particular historical title.
classic.pedia.concepts.great-works-and-archaeology.p3 = Some buildings can gain a theming bonus from a suitable collection. Review the building's requirements and the theme status shown in Culture. Excavations also offer choices there when a dig is ready.

## Trade Routes
classic.pedia.concepts.trade-routes.title = Trade Routes
classic.pedia.concepts.trade-routes.p1 = Trade units establish routes between eligible cities. Select a trade unit and open its route action to compare destinations, route kinds and the benefits currently offered.
classic.pedia.concepts.trade-routes.p2 = Internal routes support your own cities, while international routes connect you with others. Requirements depend on the route and its origin, so a city may offer some route kinds but not others.
classic.pedia.concepts.trade-routes.p3 = Use the Trade overview to inspect active routes and their remaining duration. Protect their paths and consider diplomatic conditions before relying on a route's continued income.

## City-States
classic.pedia.concepts.city-states.title = City-States
classic.pedia.concepts.city-states.p1 = City-states are independent powers with influence relationships, quests and benefits. Open City-States to inspect those you have met and their current relationship with you.
classic.pedia.concepts.city-states.p2 = Quests, gifts and other actions can change influence. Friendship and alliance are different relationship levels; another nation can compete for an alliance.
classic.pedia.concepts.city-states.p3 = Review the city-state's type and current benefits before spending resources. Influence can change over time, so an alliance should be monitored rather than treated as permanent ownership.

## Diplomacy
classic.pedia.concepts.diplomacy.title = Diplomacy
classic.pedia.concepts.diplomacy.p1 = Open Diplomacy to deal with nations you have met. Review their current relationship and available actions before declaring war, denouncing a rival or preparing an offer.
classic.pedia.concepts.diplomacy.p2 = Trade offers distinguish what you give from what you receive. Review immediate gold, recurring payments, resources and permissions separately; a valid proposal is not automatically an accepted agreement.
classic.pedia.concepts.diplomacy.p3 = Open Borders is directional: granting access to your territory is different from receiving access to theirs. War and other conditions can make agreements unavailable. Inspect incoming offers before accepting or rejecting them.

## World Congress and United Nations
classic.pedia.concepts.world-congress-and-united-nations.title = World Congress and United Nations
classic.pedia.concepts.world-congress-and-united-nations.p1 = The World Congress brings nations together to propose and vote on resolutions. Open Congress to inspect members, current proposals, voting opportunities and active effects.
classic.pedia.concepts.world-congress-and-united-nations.p2 = A proposal and a vote are separate actions. Check the available delegates and the proposal's target before committing votes; resolutions can affect more than the nation that proposed them.
classic.pedia.concepts.world-congress-and-united-nations.p3 = The United Nations introduces the World Leader vote used for diplomatic victory. The Victory screen shows the current goal and progress; merely holding delegates is not the same as having won that vote.

## Espionage
classic.pedia.concepts.espionage.title = Espionage
classic.pedia.concepts.espionage.p1 = Open Espionage to inspect available spies, their assignments and progress. Assign a spy to an eligible destination and role rather than assuming every city supports the same mission.
classic.pedia.concepts.espionage.p2 = When a technology theft becomes available, select from the eligible technologies shown. Spy assignments can also serve diplomatic or city-state purposes, depending on the role and destination.
classic.pedia.concepts.espionage.p3 = Coup attempts and other hostile operations carry consequences. Review the displayed opportunity and current relationship before ordering one; an available action does not promise success.

## Victory Conditions
classic.pedia.concepts.victory-conditions.title = Victory Conditions
classic.pedia.concepts.victory-conditions.p1 = Open Victory to see the conditions enabled for this match and your progress toward each one. Disabled conditions cannot win the current game.
classic.pedia.concepts.victory-conditions.p2 = Domination concerns original capitals. Science requires the Apollo Program and the required spaceship parts. Cultural victory requires influence over the other nations, while diplomatic victory requires winning the United Nations' World Leader vote.
classic.pedia.concepts.victory-conditions.p3 = Time victory uses the score when the configured turn limit is reached. Follow the live progress shown for each condition; the same city, technology or diplomatic action can support several long-term plans.

## Unit Combat Strength
classic.pedia.concepts.unit-combat-strength.title = Unit Combat Strength
classic.pedia.concepts.unit-combat-strength.p1 = A unit's listed strength is the starting point for combat, not a guarantee of the outcome. Terrain, health, promotions, defensive preparations and the units involved can change a fight.
classic.pedia.concepts.unit-combat-strength.p2 = Select a unit and inspect the combat preview before attacking a visible target. Compare the expected result with the position the unit will occupy afterward.
classic.pedia.concepts.unit-combat-strength.p3 = Different unit kinds suit different jobs. Use their Encyclopedia pages and promotion descriptions to understand their advantages instead of comparing strength alone.

## Combat Damage
classic.pedia.concepts.combat-damage.title = Combat Damage
classic.pedia.concepts.combat-damage.p1 = Combat reduces health and can destroy a unit. Melee exchanges can hurt both sides, so an attack that damages the enemy may also leave your own unit exposed.
classic.pedia.concepts.combat-damage.p2 = The combat preview helps you judge a particular attack. Its context matters: moving, changing targets or gaining a modifier can change the result you should expect.
classic.pedia.concepts.combat-damage.p3 = After combat, inspect the survivors and their remaining orders. Plan safe positions for damaged units and leave room for support or retreat.

## Ranged Combat
classic.pedia.concepts.ranged-combat.title = Ranged Combat
classic.pedia.concepts.ranged-combat.p1 = Ranged units attack eligible targets from a distance. Range, visibility, terrain and the unit's preparation can affect whether a particular attack is available.
classic.pedia.concepts.ranged-combat.p2 = Select the unit and inspect the target preview. Some siege units must set up before firing; moving can remove that preparation. A target nearby on the map is not necessarily a legal ranged target.
classic.pedia.concepts.ranged-combat.p3 = Ranged attacks can weaken a city or defender, but ranged fire alone does not capture a city. Arrange an eligible capturing unit as part of an assault.

## Cities in Combat
classic.pedia.concepts.cities-in-combat.title = Cities in Combat
classic.pedia.concepts.cities-in-combat.p1 = Cities have defenses and health, and can participate in combat. Defensive buildings and other effects can make an apparently small city harder to take.
classic.pedia.concepts.cities-in-combat.p2 = Inspect city attacks and combat previews when available. Weaken an enemy city before committing a capturing unit, and account for enemy units that may counterattack.
classic.pedia.concepts.cities-in-combat.p3 = Capturing a city changes more than the front line. Review its ownership, condition and management after conquest, and consider whether the captured city includes an original capital needed for domination.

## Healing
classic.pedia.concepts.healing.title = Healing
classic.pedia.concepts.healing.p1 = Damaged units need time and suitable conditions to recover. Movement, actions, location and special effects can affect whether a unit heals and how much it recovers.
classic.pedia.concepts.healing.p2 = Use the Heal order when it is available, then inspect the unit's health on later turns. Healing is not immediate restoration and does not make a unit safe from an enemy attack.
classic.pedia.concepts.healing.p3 = Fortification helps a unit hold a position, while healing concerns its health. Check both its defensive situation and its recovery rather than treating those goals as interchangeable.

## Experience and Promotions
classic.pedia.concepts.experience-and-promotions.title = Experience and Promotions
classic.pedia.concepts.experience-and-promotions.p1 = Units can gain experience and become eligible for promotions. Select a unit to inspect the promotions currently available to it and their effects.
classic.pedia.concepts.experience-and-promotions.p2 = Promotion paths have prerequisites, and some choices suit particular terrain, targets or roles. Compare the unit's intended job with the offered benefits before selecting one.
classic.pedia.concepts.experience-and-promotions.p3 = Experience belongs to a particular unit. Preserving experienced troops can be useful even when a newly available unit kind has a stronger base statistic.

## Movement
classic.pedia.concepts.movement.title = Movement
classic.pedia.concepts.movement.p1 = Select a unit, choose Move and inspect its route toward the destination. Terrain, rivers, routes, borders and other units can affect where it may travel and how far it can go this turn.
classic.pedia.concepts.movement.p2 = A path may span several turns. Check its preview and the surroundings before committing, especially near enemies or territory where access may change.
classic.pedia.concepts.movement.p3 = Land and sea units have different travel restrictions, and crossing between land and water can require special capabilities. Moving can also end fortification or other preparations, so finish a move where the unit can remain safely.

## One Unit per Tile
classic.pedia.concepts.one-unit-per-tile.title = One Unit per Tile
classic.pedia.concepts.one-unit-per-tile.p1 = Units cannot all share the same destination freely. Stacking restrictions distinguish unit roles, and a tile occupied by a friendly unit may block another unit of the same kind of role.
classic.pedia.concepts.one-unit-per-tile.p2 = Civilian and military units can share some positions, allowing escorts, but that does not remove the need to protect civilians. Enemy units and the destination's terrain add further restrictions.
classic.pedia.concepts.one-unit-per-tile.p3 = Use the movement preview to check a destination. Move blocking units or choose another route when an order cannot finish where you intended; narrow passages and crowded cities deserve extra planning.

## Air Units
classic.pedia.concepts.air-units.title = Air Units
classic.pedia.concepts.air-units.p1 = Air units operate from suitable bases rather than walking across adjacent terrain like land units. Their role, range and available orders determine which missions they can perform.
classic.pedia.concepts.air-units.p2 = Inspect the selected aircraft's orders and target preview. Interception is a defensive assignment; offensive missions and relocation have their own requirements. Not every aircraft has every air capability.
classic.pedia.concepts.air-units.p3 = Enemy air defenses can threaten a mission even when the target itself looks vulnerable. Check the aircraft's health and base situation before relying on repeated attacks.
